re.</em></figcaption></figure></div><p></p><p>The new Qwen3 Next architectu=
re stands out because, despite being 3x smaller than the previous 235B-A22B=
 model, it introduces four times as many experts and even adds a shared exp=
ert. Both of these design choices (a high expert count and the inclusion of=
 a shared expert).</p><p><span>=E2=80=8BThe other highlight is that they re=
place the regular attention mechanism with a</span><a href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/abs/2412.06464"> Gated DeltaNet</a><span> +</span><a href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/abs/2505.06708"> Gated Attention</a><span> hybrid, which helps ena=
ble the native 262k token context length in terms of memory usage (the 235B=
-A22B model supported 32k natively and 131k with</span><a href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/abs/2309.00071"> YaRN</a><span> scaling).</span></p><p>=E2=80=8BSo=
 how does this new attention hybrid work? Compared to grouped=E2=80=91query=
 attention (GQA), which is still standard scaled dot=E2=80=91product attent=
ion (sharing K/V across query=E2=80=91head groups to cut KV=E2=80=91cache s=
ize and memory bandwidth as discussed earlier, but whose decode cost and ca=
che still grow with sequence length), their hybrid mechanism mixes Gated De=
ltaNet blocks with Gated Attention blocks in a 3:1 ratio as shown in Figure=
 17.</p><div class=3D"captioned-image-container"><figure><a target=3D"_blan=
